g\">28 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong>93 record highs</strong>, close to the 75 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (48). Birmingham's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on July 29, 1930.","t_chg":"Birmingham's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 129 complete years: Birmingham's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.09\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.16 to \u22120.01), about \u22121.1\u00b0F since 1897. Birmingham's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.11\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.17), about +1.4\u00b0F since 1897. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.11\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.83 to +1.39) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.51\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.86). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months; January fell. Highs rose in 4 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Birmingham's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.35 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 56.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1929 (81.82 in); the driest, 2007 (28.86 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}